Using VS Code for my first Python assignment was a learning experience. Initially, getting familiar with VS Code, the Codio virtual machine, and GitHub was challenging. It made learning how to write and execute my code more difficult at first. However, once I became more comfortable navigating the different programs, I started to understand how the IDE features could make writing, testing, and troubleshooting code easier.

One feature I found helpful was the code formatting and syntax highlighting when importing the datetime module. The assignment required me to use the current year instead of hardcoding a specific year into my program. Using import datetime allowed me to get the current year automatically. VS Code made it easier to write and format the code correctly by highlighting different parts of the syntax. This helped me understand how the different commands worked together and made writing my current_year code easier.

The second feature I found extremely convenient was the automatic code suggestions while writing my syntax. As I started typing commands, VS Code would anticipate what I was trying to write and offer suggestions or complete parts of the code for me. When I was writing the code to retrieve the current year, this feature helped save time and made writing the program much easier. I also found this helpful compared to the practice modules, where I had to remember and manually enter the code needed for each challenge.

The third feature I found useful was the integrated terminal and Python execution. When I wanted to test my program, I could run the code directly through VS Code and see the results in the terminal. When I ran into syntax errors and other problems, the terminal displayed error messages that helped me identify where something was going wrong. I had some trouble getting the datetime module to work correctly, so being able to run the code, review the errors, make changes, and test it again was very helpful. This made troubleshooting much easier and helped me understand that fixing errors is part of programming.

Overall, my experience using VS Code helped me become more comfortable with writing and running Python programs. Initially, learning how to navigate the IDE and virtual machine was challenging, but the features made the actual coding process much easier once I understood how to use them. The code formatting, automatic suggestions, and integrated terminal were especially useful. I can see how these features will help me as I continue learning Python and work on more advanced programming assignments.

